\title[Rank-Grid Resolution in Threshold Statistics]{Rank-Grid Resolution in Threshold Statistics:\\ Exact Splitting, Sharp Nested Bounds, and the\\ Identifiability Limit of a Two-Cell Window Ladder}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{3 September 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A threshold (``gate'') statistic evaluated on a finite window of $N$ items can only be realised at the rank rates $k/N$. Consequently a measured \emph{gate drop} --- the change in a response when a threshold is hardened --- mixes an intrinsic property of the population with an artifact of the rank grid. We build an order-theoretic model of this mixing, in which a nominal tail rate $\theta$ is realised as $\gr_N(\theta)=\lceil\theta N\rceil/N$, and derive an exact decomposition of the measured drop into an intrinsic drop and two resolution residuals.

Four groups of results follow. \emph{(i) Structure and consistency:} the residual of an antitone response is nonnegative, decreases under grid refinement, is bounded by $L/N$ for an $L$-Lipschitz response, and the measured response converges to the ideal one. \emph{(ii) Cross-window accounting:} in a \emph{decoupled} design (gates held fixed across windows) the intrinsic drop cancels identically and the cross-window difference $D$ is a pure resolution quantity, with $|D|\le 2L(1/N_1+1/N_2)$ in general and the sharper, fine-window-free bound $|D|\le L/N_1$ for \emph{nested} windows $N_2=N_1c$; an explicit linear witness attains $D=L/320$ at $(240,960)$, so the resulting slope certificate recovers exactly $3/4$ of the truth. A positive nested $D$ necessarily moves the hard gate between grids, and the refinement changes the realised gate with probability $1-1/c$ for a uniformly placed gate. In a \emph{nested} design the gate drift $\varepsilon$ contributes an additional $2L(\varepsilon+1/N_2)$, so sample size and bound growth are not separable. \emph{(iii) Derivation of the $1/N$ law:} averaging over the position of the gate inside its rank cell gives an offset-averaged residual of exactly $L/(2N)$ --- the constant is half the local slope, not a fitted parameter --- and the offset-averaged bias of a drop is $(L_2-L_1)/(2N)$, so that $D>0$ holds \emph{if and only if} the response is steeper at the hard gate than at the soft gate. \emph{(iv) An identifiability limit:} we exhibit two antitone $L$-Lipschitz responses that every cell of a two-cell nested ladder measures \emph{identically}, yet whose intrinsic drops differ by exactly $L/(4N_{\mathrm{fine}})$.

Applied to a reported four-cell experiment with $\Delta(240)=0.1073$, $\Delta(960)=0.0636$, $D=+0.0437$ (interval $[0.0346,0.0533]$), the theory yields a certified Lipschitz floor $L\ge 8.30$; a proof that both pre-stated hypotheses fail across the entire reported confidence box; a Richardson-extrapolated intrinsic level $I=0.0490$, i.e.\ a resolution share of $54\%$ rather than the reported $41\%$; an intrinsic share pinned only to $[0.36,0.60]$; a falsifiable third-cell prediction $\Delta(3840)\in[0.0459,0.0607]$; and a structural ambiguity of only $\approx 0.0022$, about $2\%$ of $\Delta(240)$. The last number decides the design question: the wide share interval is statistical width, not a resolution limit. More seeds, not finer windows.
\end{abstract}

