\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The empirical situation}

Consider a response measured on the extreme tail of a scored population. A gate at nominal tail rate $\theta$ admits the top $\theta$-fraction of items; hardening the gate from a soft level $\theta_1$ to a hard level $\theta_2>\theta_1$ produces a \emph{gate drop}. The design that motivates this paper is a $2\times 2$ factorial: two window sizes $N\in\{240,960\}$ crossed with two threshold levels, replicated over eight populations. It reports:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{rll}
\toprule
window $N$ & measured drop $\Delta(N)$ & interval \\
\midrule
$240$ & $+0.1073$ & $[0.0973,\,0.1148]$ \\
$960$ & $+0.0636$ & $[0.0597,\,0.0680]$ \\
$D=\Delta(240)-\Delta(960)$ & $+0.0437$ & $[0.0346,\,0.0533]$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Two hypotheses were stated before the data were seen: (H1) \emph{most} of the drop is a resolution artifact, so quadrupling the window should recover most of it; and (H2) \emph{none} of it is, so window size is irrelevant. The outcome is \textbf{neither}: quadrupling recovers $D/\Delta(240)\approx 41\%$, a real but minority fraction.

\subsection{What this paper does}

Nothing below is statistics. Every empirical number enters only as a hypothesis on a real variable, and every conclusion is a theorem about those hypotheses. The contribution is a model in which ``resolution component'' is a \emph{definite} mathematical object rather than a residual to be fitted, plus the sharp two-sided accounting the model makes possible.

The organising question is not \emph{what was measured} but \emph{what can this design know}. We answer with matching bounds: an upper bound on the ambiguity, $|D|\le L/N_1$ for nested windows (\S\ref{sec:nested}), and a lower bound, $L/(4N_{\mathrm{fine}})$, realised by two responses the design cannot separate (\S\ref{sec:ident}).

Throughout, $S:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ is a \emph{response}: the quantity of interest as a function of the nominal tail rate. $N,M,c,N_1,N_2$ are positive integers; $\theta,\theta_1,\theta_2$ are nominal rates with $\theta_1<\theta_2$; $L\ge0$ is a slope budget. Windows with $N_2=N_1c$ are called \emph{nested}.

\section{The rank-grid model}

\begin{definition}[Realised gate]
The \textbf{realised gate} of a nominal rate $\theta$ on a window of $N$ items is
\[ \gr_N(\theta) \;=\; \frac{\lceil \theta N\rceil}{N}. \]
\end{definition}

Only the rates $k/N$ occur on a window of $N$ items; the definition rounds a request \emph{up} to the next realisable rate, the conservative convention for a tail gate.

\begin{definition}[Lipschitz budget]
$S$ has \textbf{Lipschitz budget} $L$ if $|S(x)-S(y)|\le L|x-y|$ for all $x,y$.
\end{definition}

A Lipschitz budget is automatically nonnegative, since $0\le|S(0)-S(1)|\le L$.

\begin{definition}[Measured response, residual, drops]\label{def:split}
The \textbf{measured response} is $S_N(\theta)=S(\gr_N(\theta))$; the \textbf{resolution residual} is $r_N(\theta)=S(\theta)-S_N(\theta)$; the \textbf{measured gate drop} is $\Delta(N)=S_N(\theta_1)-S_N(\theta_2)$; the \textbf{intrinsic gate drop} is $\Delta(\infty)=S(\theta_1)-S(\theta_2)$; and the \textbf{cross-window difference} is $D=\Delta(N_1)-\Delta(N_2)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Basic geometry of the grid]\label{prop:grid}
For every $N\ge1$ and $\theta,\theta'\in\mathbb{R}$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\theta \le \gr_N(\theta) < \theta + 1/N$; hence $|\gr_N(\theta)-\theta|\le 1/N$.
\item $\gr_N$ is monotone in $\theta$.
\item $|\gr_N(\theta)-\gr_N(\theta')| \le |\theta-\theta'| + 1/N$.
\item (\textbf{Refinement}) For every $c\ge1$, $\gr_{Nc}(\theta)\le\gr_N(\theta)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
(1) is $\lceil x\rceil\ge x$ and $\lceil x\rceil<x+1$ at $x=\theta N$, divided by $N$. (2) is monotonicity of $\lceil\cdot\rceil$. (3) follows from (1) at both points. For (4): $\theta N\le\lceil\theta N\rceil$ gives $\theta\cdot Nc\le\lceil\theta N\rceil c$, and $\lceil\theta N\rceil c$ is an integer, so $\lceil\theta Nc\rceil\le\lceil\theta N\rceil c$; divide by $Nc$.
\end{proof}

Item (4) is the structural fact that the design's windows satisfy $960=240\cdot4$, and it is worth a factor $2.5$ in \S\ref{sec:nested}.

\begin{proposition}[Residual structure]\label{prop:resid}
Let $S$ be antitone and $N\ge1$. Then $r_N(\theta)\ge0$; $r_{Nc}(\theta)\le r_N(\theta)$ for all $c\ge1$; if $S$ has Lipschitz budget $L$ (antitonicity not needed) then $|r_N(\theta)|\le L/N$; and in that case $S_N(\theta)\to S(\theta)$ as $N\to\infty$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Nonnegativity: $\theta\le\gr_N(\theta)$ and $S$ antitone. Refinement: apply $S$ antitone to Proposition~\ref{prop:grid}(4). Magnitude: $|r_N(\theta)|\le L|\gr_N(\theta)-\theta|\le L/N$. Consistency: squeeze $|r_N|$ between $0$ and $L/N\to0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact split of a measured drop]\label{thm:split}
For every response $S$, window $N$ and gates $\theta_1,\theta_2$,
\[ \Delta(N) \;=\; \Delta(\infty) \;-\; r_N(\theta_1) \;+\; r_N(\theta_2). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Substitute $S(\gr_N\theta_i)=S(\theta_i)-r_N(\theta_i)$ into $\Delta(N)=S(\gr_N\theta_1)-S(\gr_N\theta_2)$.
\end{proof}

This is an identity, not an approximation: it has no error term.

\section{Decoupled designs measure pure resolution}

The design's named follow-up is to \emph{decouple the strip bound from the population median}, i.e.\ to hold both gates fixed across the two windows.

\begin{theorem}[Decoupled cross-window difference is pure resolution]\label{thm:pure}
If the gates are held fixed across windows, then
\[ D \;=\; \bigl(r_{N_2}(\theta_1)-r_{N_1}(\theta_1)\bigr) \;-\; \bigl(r_{N_2}(\theta_2)-r_{N_1}(\theta_2)\bigr). \]
In particular $D$ contains no information whatsoever about $\Delta(\infty)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:split} at $N_1$ and $N_2$ and subtract; $\Delta(\infty)$ is the same real number in both and cancels identically.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Generic size bound]\label{cor:generic}
If $S$ has Lipschitz budget $L$ then $|D|\le 2L\left(\frac{1}{N_1}+\frac{1}{N_2}\right)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Nested designs are confounded, quantitatively]\label{thm:drift}
Suppose window $N_1$ uses gates $(a_1,a_2)$ and window $N_2$ uses gates $(b_1,b_2)$ with drift $|a_i-b_i|\le\varepsilon$. Then
\[ \bigl|\Delta_S(N_1;a_1,a_2)-\Delta_S(N_2;b_1,b_2)\bigr| \;\le\; 2L\left(\frac{1}{N_1}+\frac{1}{N_2}\right)+2L\left(\varepsilon+\frac{1}{N_2}\right). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Insert and subtract $\Delta_S(N_2;a_1,a_2)$. The first difference is decoupled and bounded by Corollary~\ref{cor:generic}. For the second, Proposition~\ref{prop:grid}(3) moves each realised gate by at most $\varepsilon+1/N_2$, and the Lipschitz budget converts this into $L(\varepsilon+1/N_2)$ per gate.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
In a nested design, where the strip bound grows with the window, sample size and bound growth are not separable: a nonzero $D$ can be produced by gate drift alone. This is the experiment's own recorded caveat, made quantitative, and the formal reason the decoupled follow-up changes what is being measured rather than merely refining it.
\end{remark}

\section{Sharp accounting for nested windows}\label{sec:nested}

\begin{theorem}[Nested cross-window bound]\label{thm:nested}
Let $S$ be antitone with Lipschitz budget $L$, and $N_2=N_1c$ with $c\ge1$. Then $|D|\le L/N_1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:pure}, $D=B_1-B_2$ with $B_i=r_{N_2}(\theta_i)-r_{N_1}(\theta_i)$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:resid}, $0\le r_{N_2}(\theta_i)\le r_{N_1}(\theta_i)\le L/N_1$, so $B_i\in[-L/N_1,0]$ and $D\in[-L/N_1,L/N_1]$.
\end{proof}

Note the absence of $N_2$: refining the fine window cannot widen what the comparison sees. The coarse window is the bottleneck.

\begin{corollary}[Certified slope floor]\label{cor:floor}
At $(N_1,N_2)=(240,960)$ with $D\ge0.0346$: $L\ge 240\times0.0346=8.30$.
\end{corollary}

The generic bound reads $|D|\le L/96$ and gives only $L\ge 3.32$; nesting sharpens the certificate by a factor $2.5$. Either way, \emph{a flat response cannot produce the reported $D$}: something in the strip is genuinely steep.

\begin{theorem}[An explicit witness, and the $4/3$ gap]\label{thm:witness}
Let $S(x)=-Lx$ with $L\ge0$ --- antitone, Lipschitz budget $L$, with no intrinsic window dependence of any kind. With gates $\theta_1=0$ and $\theta_2=1/960$,
\[ \Delta(240)=\frac{L}{240},\qquad \Delta(960)=\frac{L}{960},\qquad D=\frac{L}{320}>0, \]
and the certificate of Corollary~\ref{cor:floor} returns $240\,D=\tfrac34L$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\gr_N(0)=0$; $\gr_{240}(1/960)=\lceil 1/4\rceil/240=1/240$; $\gr_{960}(1/960)=1/960$.
\end{proof}

Two conclusions. First, \textbf{$D>0$ certifies nothing intrinsic by itself}: a purely linear response with no window dependence reproduces $D>0$ exactly, so the implicit inference ``the effect survives quadrupling, therefore resolution is a minority'' is invalid. Second, Theorems~\ref{thm:nested} and \ref{thm:witness} bracket the design's power to recover the slope within the factor $4/3$ --- exactly the price of not knowing where inside its rank cell the gate sits.

\begin{proposition}[Structural necessary condition]\label{prop:hardgate}
Let $S$ be antitone and $N_2=N_1c$. If $D>0$ then $\gr_{N_2}(\theta_2)<\gr_{N_1}(\theta_2)$: the refinement must move the \emph{hard} gate. Contrapositively, if the hard gate is realised identically on both windows then $D\le0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
From $D=B_1-B_2>0$ and $B_1\le0$ we get $B_2<0$, so the realised hard gates differ; Proposition~\ref{prop:grid}(4) fixes the direction.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[How often refinement matters]\label{prop:agree}
For $\theta$ in the first coarse cell $(0,1/M]$, one has $\gr_{Mc}(\theta)=\gr_M(\theta)$ if and only if $\theta>\tfrac1M-\tfrac1{Mc}$. The agreement set is an interval of length $\tfrac1{Mc}$, so a uniformly placed gate is \emph{moved} by the refinement with probability $1-\tfrac1c$; for $240\to960$ this is exactly $3/4$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
On $(0,1/M]$, $\lceil\theta M\rceil=1$, so $\gr_M(\theta)=1/M$. Equality with $\lceil\theta Mc\rceil/(Mc)$ holds iff $\lceil\theta Mc\rceil=c$, iff $\theta Mc>c-1$, iff $\theta>\tfrac{c-1}{Mc}$. The agreement set $\left(\tfrac1M-\tfrac1{Mc},\tfrac1M\right]$ has Lebesgue measure $\tfrac1{Mc}$.
\end{proof}

\section{Deriving the $1/N$ law by offset averaging}\label{sec:offset}

\begin{definition}[Cell average]
The \textbf{offset average} of $f$ across one rank cell of width $1/N$ is $\avg{f}_N=N\int_0^{1/N}f(t)\,dt$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Granularity destroys offset information]\label{lem:const}
For $t\in(0,1/N]$ and any integer $k$, $\gr_N\!\left(\tfrac kN+t\right)=\tfrac{k+1}{N}$. Hence $\avg{t\mapsto S_N(\tfrac kN+t)}_N=S\!\left(\tfrac{k+1}{N}\right)$, a constant in the offset.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\left(\tfrac kN+t\right)N=tN+k$ with $tN\in(0,1]$, so the ceiling is $k+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[The smooth response averages to its cell midpoint]\label{lem:mid}
If $S(x)=A-Lx$ on $[\tfrac kN,\tfrac{k+1}{N}]$, then $\avg{t\mapsto S(\tfrac kN+t)}_N=A-L\left(\tfrac kN+\tfrac1{2N}\right)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$N\int_0^{1/N}(C-Lt)\,dt = C-\tfrac{L}{2N}$ with $C=A-Lk/N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The $1/N$ law, with its constant]\label{thm:law}
Under the hypothesis of Lemma~\ref{lem:mid},
\[ \avg{t\mapsto r_N\!\left(\tfrac kN+t\right)}_N \;=\; \frac{L}{2N}. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Subtract Lemma~\ref{lem:const} from Lemma~\ref{lem:mid}: $\tfrac LN-\tfrac{L}{2N}=\tfrac{L}{2N}$.
\end{proof}

The ansatz ``residual $=c/N$'' is therefore correct, and $c=L/2$ is \emph{half the local slope}, not a fitted parameter.

\begin{theorem}[Offset-averaged bias of a drop]\label{thm:bias}
Let $S$ be locally affine with slope $-L_1$ across the soft gate's cell and $-L_2$ across the hard gate's cell. Then the offset-averaged measured drop exceeds the offset-averaged intrinsic drop by exactly $\dfrac{L_2-L_1}{2N}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Sign rigidity]\label{cor:sign}
On the offset-averaged model, the drop shrinks as $N$ grows --- the reported $D>0$ --- \emph{if and only if} $L_1<L_2$: the response is steeper at the hard gate than at the soft gate.
\end{corollary}

For a survival curve over a tail with a decaying density the response \emph{flattens} outward, $L_2<L_1$, forcing $D<0$. The reported $D=+0.0437$ therefore has the \emph{wrong sign} to be explained by rank granularity acting on a decaying tail. The remaining explanations are gate drift in the nested design (Theorem~\ref{thm:drift}) or genuine threshold reweighting.

\section{Auditing the reported four-cell reading}

\begin{theorem}[Both pre-stated hypotheses fail, on the whole box]\label{thm:neither}
Write $\Delta(240)=I+r_1$ and $\Delta(960)=I+r_2$ with $r_2\ge0$ and $\Delta(240)>0$. If $\Delta(240)\le0.1148$, $\Delta(960)\ge0.0597$ and $D\ge0.0346$, then $r_1>0$ and $D/\Delta(240)<\tfrac12$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$D=r_1-r_2\ge0.0346>0$ and $r_2\ge0$ give $r_1>0$, so H2 fails. And $2D<\Delta(240)$ is equivalent to $\Delta(240)<2\Delta(960)$, which holds since $\Delta(240)\le0.1148<0.1194=2(0.0597)\le2\Delta(960)$; divide by $\Delta(240)>0$, so H1 fails.
\end{proof}

The \textbf{NEITHER} verdict is thus robust across the entire reported confidence box, not a point-estimate artifact. What follows shows that the \emph{share} is not.

\begin{theorem}[Richardson extrapolation]\label{thm:rich}
If $\Delta(N)=I+c/N$, then
\[ I=\frac{4\Delta(960)-\Delta(240)}{3}, \qquad \frac{c}{240}=\frac43\bigl(\Delta(240)-\Delta(960)\bigr)=\frac43D. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[The reported recovery understates the share by exactly $4/3$]
$\dfrac{c/240}{\Delta(240)}=\dfrac43\cdot\dfrac{D}{\Delta(240)}$. Numerically $\tfrac43\times41\%\approx54\%$: the resolution share of the coarse cell implied by the $1/N$ model is a \emph{majority}, not the reported minority. The fine cell still carries a quarter of the coarse residual, so the between-cell recovery necessarily misses a quarter of it.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[At the point estimates the headline is reversed]
With $\Delta(240)=0.1073$ and $\Delta(960)=0.0636$, Theorem~\ref{thm:rich} gives $I=0.04903\overline{3}$, so $0<I$ and $2I<\Delta(240)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Interval for the intrinsic share]\label{thm:share}
Assume $\Delta(N)=I+c/N$. If $\Delta(240)\in[0.0973,0.1148]$ and
$\Delta(960)\in[0.0597,0.0680]$, then
\[ \frac{9}{25}\Delta(240)\;\le\;I\;\le\;\frac35\Delta(240), \qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad \frac{I}{\Delta(240)}\in[0.36,\,0.60]. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$3I=4\Delta(960)-\Delta(240)$, so both claims are linear. Upper: $I\le\tfrac35\Delta(240)$ is $4\Delta(960)\le\tfrac{14}{5}\Delta(240)$, and $4(0.0680)=0.2720\le0.27244=2.8(0.0973)$. Lower: $I\ge\tfrac9{25}\Delta(240)$ is $4\Delta(960)\ge\tfrac{52}{25}\Delta(240)$, and $4(0.0597)=0.23880\ge0.238784=2.08(0.1148)$.
\end{proof}

Both bounds are tight to four decimal places on the reported box. \textbf{``Mostly intrinsic'' ($>1/2$) is consistent with the four cells but not certified by them}: the interval $[0.36,0.60]$ straddles $1/2$.

\begin{theorem}[Falsifiable third-cell prediction]\label{thm:third}
Assume $\Delta(N)=I+c/N$.  Then
\[ \Delta(3840)=\frac{5\Delta(960)-\Delta(240)}{4}, \]
numerically $0.0527$, confined by the reported intervals to $[0.0459,0.0607]$.
\end{theorem}

A measured $\Delta(3840)$ outside that interval refutes the $1/N$ reading, and with it the $41\%$-versus-$54\%$ arithmetic.

\section{The identifiability limit of a two-cell ladder}\label{sec:ident}

All of the above bounds the resolution component \emph{from above}. The complementary question is a \textbf{lower bound on ignorance}.

\begin{definition}[Reference and adversary]
Fix $L\ge0$ and set
\[ \ell(x)=-\frac L2x, \qquad
\kappa(x)=\begin{cases}
-Lx, & x\le\tfrac1{1920},\\[2pt]
-\tfrac{L}{1920}, & \tfrac1{1920}<x\le\tfrac1{960},\\[2pt]
-\tfrac L2x, & x>\tfrac1{960}.
\end{cases} \]
\end{definition}

The adversary $\kappa$ descends at the \emph{maximal admissible} rate $-L$ on the first half of the first fine cell, is flat on the second half, and rejoins $\ell$ from $1/960$ onwards.

\begin{lemma}[Both responses are admissible]
For $L\ge0$, both $\ell$ and $\kappa$ are antitone with Lipschitz budget $L$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For $\ell$, $\ell(x)-\ell(y)=-\tfrac L2(x-y)$ and $L/2\le L$. For $\kappa$, a case analysis on which pieces contain $x\le y$ gives $0\le\kappa(x)-\kappa(y)\le L(y-x)$ in every case, which yields both claims. The three slopes $-L$, $0$, $-L/2$ all lie in $[-L,0]$, and the pieces match continuously at $1/1920$ and $1/960$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[The three rates the design ever evaluates]\label{lem:rates}
With gates $\theta_1=0$ and $\theta_2=1/1920$:
\[ \gr_{240}(0)=\gr_{960}(0)=0,\qquad \gr_{240}\!\left(\tfrac1{1920}\right)=\tfrac1{240},\qquad \gr_{960}\!\left(\tfrac1{1920}\right)=\tfrac1{960}. \]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\lceil 1/8\rceil=1$ and $\lceil 1/2\rceil=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Indistinguishability]\label{thm:indist}
$\kappa$ and $\ell$ agree at $0$, $1/240$ and $1/960$. Consequently, for gates $(0,1/1920)$, $\Delta_\kappa(240)=\Delta_\ell(240)$ and $\Delta_\kappa(960)=\Delta_\ell(960)$: every cell of the design returns the same number for the two responses.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\kappa(0)=0=\ell(0)$; at $1/240>1/960$ the third branch of $\kappa$ \emph{is} $\ell$; and $\kappa(1/960)=-L/1920=-\tfrac L2\cdot\tfrac1{960}=\ell(1/960)$. Combine with Lemma~\ref{lem:rates}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Yet the intrinsic drops differ]\label{thm:gap}
$\Delta_\kappa(\infty)-\Delta_\ell(\infty)=\dfrac{L}{3840}=\dfrac{L}{4\cdot960}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both vanish at $0$; at the hard gate $\kappa(1/1920)=-L/1920$ while $\ell(1/1920)=-L/3840$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Identifiability limit]\label{thm:ident}
For every $L\ge0$ there exist two antitone responses with Lipschitz budget $L$ that are measured identically by both cells of the two-cell nested ladder, yet have intrinsic drops differing by exactly $L/(4N_{\mathrm{fine}})$ with $N_{\mathrm{fine}}=960$. Hence no re-analysis of the four cells can resolve the intrinsic drop below that width; conversely Theorem~\ref{thm:nested} shows the design does resolve it to within $L/N_{\mathrm{coarse}}$, a constant multiple.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[The wide share interval is statistical, not structural]\label{cor:triage}
With the certified floor $L\ge8.30$ of Corollary~\ref{cor:floor} as the operative slope scale, the structural ambiguity is $L/3840\approx0.0022$, about $2\%$ of $\Delta(240)=0.1073$. The intrinsic-share interval of Theorem~\ref{thm:share} has width $0.24$, an order of magnitude larger. The $[0.36,0.60]$ spread is therefore statistical width in the reported confidence intervals, not a resolution limit of the design. More seeds, not finer windows.
\end{corollary}

\section{Algorithms}

The theory is fully constructive.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Grid realisation and residual evaluation.} Given a response oracle $S$, a window $N$ and a rate $\theta$, return $\lceil\theta N\rceil/N$, $S$ of it, and the residual. Cost $O(1)$.
\item \textbf{Nested slope certificate.} From nested $N_1\mid N_2$ and a measured $D^-$, return $L\ge N_1D^-$. Correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:nested}; Theorem~\ref{thm:witness} shows this recovers $\tfrac{N_2-N_1}{N_2}L$ on the linear witness, i.e.\ $\tfrac34L$ at $(240,960)$. Cost $O(1)$.
\item \textbf{Richardson intrinsic extrapolation with interval propagation.} From $\Delta(N_1)$, $\Delta(N_2)$ with $N_2=cN_1$, return $I=\frac{c\Delta(N_2)-\Delta(N_1)}{c-1}$, the coarse resolution part, the share, and the prediction for a third nested cell. Interval propagation is exact: the map is affine and monotone in each argument, so evaluating at two opposite corners suffices. Cost $O(1)$.
\item \textbf{Structural-versus-statistical triage.} Compare $L/(4N_2)$ as a fraction of $\Delta(N_1)$ against the statistical width of the share interval, and recommend replication or resolution accordingly. This is the operational content of Corollary~\ref{cor:triage}.
\item \textbf{Offset-averaged simulator.} Sample gate offsets uniformly inside a rank cell and average the measured drop. Theorem~\ref{thm:law} predicts a mean residual of exactly $L/(2N)$; for a nonlinear response the simulator quantifies the departure.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Applications}

Any statistic defined by ``take the top $\theta$ fraction'' is subject to the rank-grid phenomenon whenever the sample is finite.

\emph{Extreme-value and risk analytics.} Value-at-risk and expected shortfall at a nominal level are computed from order statistics. Comparisons across backtesting windows are exactly the situation of Theorem~\ref{thm:pure}, and any observed level dependence should be tested against $L/N_1$ before being attributed to a regime change.

\emph{Selective inference.} Procedures cutting at a data-dependent rank inherit the same $O(1/N)$ bias, with leading term $L/(2N)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:law}.

\emph{Model evaluation at fixed selectivity.} Comparing systems ``at the top $1\%$'' on evaluation sets of different sizes compares two different realised thresholds; by Proposition~\ref{prop:agree} a $c$-fold change in set size moves the realised threshold with probability $1-1/c$.

\emph{Design of window ladders.} Theorem~\ref{thm:ident} gives, before any data are collected, the width of the design's blind spot. Comparing that number to the anticipated statistical width tells the experimenter whether to invest in replication or in resolution.

\section{Discussion}

Three findings deserve emphasis, because each contradicts a natural and wrong inference.

\emph{A nonzero window effect certifies nothing intrinsic.} Theorem~\ref{thm:witness} constructs a perfectly linear response, with no intrinsic window dependence at all, that reproduces $D>0$ exactly. Only the \emph{size} of $D$ relative to $L/N_1$ is informative.

\emph{The reported recovery is not the resolution share.} Between-cell recovery and the coarse cell's resolution share differ by exactly $c/(c-1)=4/3$ at $(240,960)$. At the point estimates this converts a $41\%$ minority into a $54\%$ majority and reverses the headline; over the reported box the intrinsic share is only pinned to $[0.36,0.60]$.

\emph{The sign is the strongest constraint available, and it points away from granularity.} Corollary~\ref{cor:sign} turns $D>0$ into the exact statement $L_2>L_1$, which fails for a decaying tail density. The live candidates are gate drift (Theorem~\ref{thm:drift}) or genuine threshold reweighting; the decoupled follow-up separates them, since Theorem~\ref{thm:pure} makes $D$ a pure residual difference and Theorem~\ref{thm:law} gives it a closed form.

What \emph{is} robust is the \textbf{NEITHER} verdict itself, established by Theorem~\ref{thm:neither} across the entire reported confidence box, independent of any modelling choice about how the residual scales.

The methodological lesson is Corollary~\ref{cor:triage}. A design has two kinds of uncertainty calling for opposite remedies: structural ambiguity, here computable and small ($\approx0.0022$), and statistical width, here large ($0.24$ on the share). Confusing them is how experimental programmes end up refining an instrument that was never the bottleneck.

\emph{Limitations.} The model assumes the response is a deterministic function of the realised tail rate, and that the empirical numbers are the model's quantities; sampling noise is not modelled, and the intervals enter as hypotheses. Local affineness is assumed only where used, and the identifiability construction uses a specific, though entirely admissible, adversary.

\section{Future directions}

\subsection{Offset-randomised gating}
Rank granularity is not noise to be averaged away but a \emph{deterministic} map $\theta\mapsto\lceil\theta N\rceil/N$ whose only free parameter is where the gate sits inside its cell --- so randomising that offset turns the resolution component into a quantity with a computable law, $L/(2N)$ per gate, instead of a residual to be fitted. Theorem~\ref{thm:pure} shows that decoupling alone makes $D$ a pure residual difference, and Theorem~\ref{thm:law} says the offset average then has a closed form, so the experiment becomes a direct measurement of the local slope rather than a share estimate.

\begin{conjecture}
In a decoupled design with the gate offset drawn uniformly inside its rank cell, $\mathbb{E}[\Delta(N)]=\Delta(\infty)+\dfrac{L_2-L_1}{2N}$ exactly, with no fitted constant.
\end{conjecture}

\subsection{Sign rigidity of the window effect}
$D>0$ is equivalent, on the offset-averaged model, to the response being steeper at the hard gate, which for a unimodal score distribution with decaying tail density is false. The reported $D=+0.0437$ therefore cannot be explained by rank granularity acting on a decaying tail.

\begin{conjecture}
For every population whose score density is decreasing across the strip between the two gates, the offset-averaged decoupled cross-window difference is nonpositive; hence any decoupled design that still reports $D>0$ certifies threshold reweighting.
\end{conjecture}

\subsection{The third cell}
Run $N=3840$. Theorem~\ref{thm:third} predicts $\Delta(3840)\in[0.0459,0.0607]$. A measurement outside falsifies the $1/N$ law and voids the share arithmetic.

\subsection{Beyond two cells}
Theorem~\ref{thm:ident} gives the blind spot of a two-cell ladder. The natural generalisation asks for the identifiability width of a $k$-cell nested ladder $N,Nc,Nc^2,\dots$; a plausible target is a width of order $L/(4Nc^{\,k-1})$, i.e.\ exponentially decreasing in the number of cells but only linearly in the total sample.

\subsection{Non-uniform grids}
Proposition~\ref{prop:grid}(4) is the only place uniformity is used essentially, and it survives whenever the fine grid \emph{contains} the coarse one. Extending the results to arbitrary nested grids should be routine and would broaden applicability.

\section{Conclusion}

Making the rank grid explicit converts a soft methodological worry into an exact calculus. A measured gate drop splits, with no error term, into an intrinsic drop and two resolution residuals. In a design that holds its gates fixed, the cross-window difference is \emph{purely} the second kind, bounded by $L/N_{\mathrm{coarse}}$ for nested windows and attained within $4/3$ by an explicit linear witness. Averaging over the gate's position in its cell derives the $1/N$ law with constant $L/2$, and turns the sign of the window effect into an exact statement about local steepness. An explicit pair of indistinguishable responses shows the design's blind spot has width exactly $L/(4N_{\mathrm{fine}})$.

Applied to the reported experiment: the \textbf{NEITHER} verdict is robust; the resolution \emph{share} is not, and at the point estimates it is a majority rather than the reported minority; the sign of the effect argues against granularity as its cause; and the structural ambiguity, $\approx2\%$ of the coarse drop, is an order of magnitude smaller than the statistical width of the share interval. The next experiment should decouple the gates and add seeds, not shrink the grid.

